Abstract
Adsorbents based on $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ for $\pi$ -complexative separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ have attracted widespread interests. However, they are still confronting some challenges, for example, (i) a low separation efficiency, resulted from the ineffective reduction of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ along with aggregation, and (ii) poor stability due to the oxidation of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ to inactive $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ . In this study, active Cu and auxiliary Co species are simultaneously encapsulated within the nanopores of MIL-101 using a double-solvent (DS) method to obtain CuCoM-DS. The Cu species at the interior of MIL-101 are homogeneously dispersed and can be completely reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ without any structural damage to MIL-101. The resulting CuCoMDS exhibits a superior performance in $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation not only to the pristine MIL-101, but to the counterpart samples of single $\mathrm{Cu}$ and/or $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ at the exterior of MIL-101. The best sample of 1.5CuCoM-DS adsorbent is capable to adsorb $50.5~\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity is 2.6 at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ . Both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity are higher than those reference samples. Moreover, 1.5CuCoM-DS preserves over $90\%$ of fresh $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake after the exposure to atmospheric air for 12 days. This study provides new design ideas for confining bimetallic sites in MOFs for broad applications.
Keywords: Gas separation; $\pi$ -Complexation; Metal organic frameworks (MOFs); Ethylene/ethane; IAST
## Introduction
Ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ is an essential raw chemical of a series of valuable products such as polymers, plastics and rubbers, and thus plays a pivotal role in petrochemical industries [1-3]. Gaseous $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures are the common products feasibly obtained from steam cracking of petroleum hydrocarbons by distillation [4-6]. In the practical processes, further separating $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ is still a significant challenge due to their similar molecular sizes and nearly the same boiling points [7-9]. In recent years, separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ in the industry mostly relies on the typical cryogenic distillation, which is carried out at a high pressure up to 23 bar and a low temperature down to $-25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and has been regarded as one of the most energy-intensive technologies [10-12]. Then, efficient $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation with low energy consumption is urgently required. To this end, adsorptive separation with the advantages of low cost, simple process, ease of operation and superb repeatability, has been brought to attention [13-15].
Adsorbent materials are the kernel in the adsorption separation of the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture, and $\pi$ - complexation adsorbents represent one of the promising families. They are usually prepared by loading active metal sites on the porous media [16-19]. The active metal sites, such as $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ and $\mathrm{Ag^{I}}$ , can form $\pi$ -complexation bonds with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . Such a bond strength is stronger than that of physical interaction with ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , thus $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ can be preferentially adsorbed on the $\pi$ -complexation adsorbents [20- 22]. In comparison with $\mathrm{Ag^{I}}$ , $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ is more cost-effective and easy to be obtained [23-25]. Therefore, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ has been widely loaded to various supports, for example, molecular sieves [26-28], activated carbon [29-31], and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) [32-34], to synthesize $\pi$ -complexation adsorbents. MOFs possessing ultra-large surface areas and pore volumes are ideal supports for loading metal sites [35-38]. Nevertheless, loading $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ on MOFs has been restricted by three issues. First, the reduction of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ is sluggish at relatively low temperatures on MOFs. Controllable reduction of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ always requires an elevated temperature above $450^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ . The condition is acceptable to most conventional supports, like mesoporous silica and zeolites [39-41], but would destroy most MOFs’ structures [42, 43]. The second problem is the poor dispersion of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ on MOFs. Though MOFs possess super-high specific surface areas, the high dispersion of $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ cannot always be achieved for granted. The tendency of metal species staying outside nanoporous MOFs and following aggregation into particles leads to the unsatisfactory $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation performance. The low stability of $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ sites is the third concern, as $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ can be very easily oxidized to $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ in humid air. This inevitably deteriorates the function of $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ $\pi$ -complexation adsorbents. As a result, the development of adsorbents by addressing above three issues is highly in demand.
Herein, we confine both active cuprous sites and auxiliary cobalt species within the nanopores of MIL-101 by using a double-solvent (DS) method to prepare CuCoM-DS adsorbents (Scheme 1). The well known DS method facilitates the $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ sites to be driven inside MIL-101, during which the confinement effect of nanopores of MIL-101 also eliminates the agglomeration of guest metal sites. Thanks to the good dispersion and Co doping, $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ can be completely reduced to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ at the temperature of as low as $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , meantime the structure of MIL-101 is well maintained. In comparison, the $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ sites in CuCoM-WI derived from conventional wet impregnation (WI), and CuCoM-SG from a solidstate grinding (SG) method are found to be incompletely reduced and formed aggregation outside of MIL-101. Another finding is that in CuM-DS without the Co species, $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ cannot be reduced to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ even inside MIL-101. It is further discovered that the resulting CuCoM-DS adsorbents show enhanced $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation performances in terms of not only $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ capacity, but also $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity, in comparison with the supporter MIL-101 and counterpart samples of CuCoM-WI, CuCoM-SG, CuM-DS. In addition, the active $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ sites in CuCoM-DS are robust in ambient condition, and their $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ capacity can maintain over $90\%$ of the fresh adsorbent after 12 days.
## Materials and methods
All the chemicals were provided by Sigma-Aldrich and Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. They were in analytical grade and used directly without additional treatment.
## Synthesis of $\mathbf{Cu/Co}$ decorated samples
MIL-101: MIL-101 with $\mathrm{Cr}$ as the central metal ion was synthesized by the classical hydrothermal method [44, 45]. In specific, $2.007\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.831\ \mathrm{g}$ of terephthalic acid (TPA) were added into $25.2\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, and then the suspension was kept stirring at ambient condition for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Afterwards, HF (six drops) was added into the suspension dropwise, and extra stirring for $2\mathrm{h}$ was following conducted. Subsequently, the mixture was decanted into a $100\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon reactor, which was then heated at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ . It was then cooled naturally, and the suspension was filtered and dried at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain the green powder. The collected powder was refluxed in DMF at 150 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight then filtered and dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, it was refluxed in ethanol at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight to remove DMF, then the crystal was filtered and dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain MIL-101. It is noted that for washing procedure, $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF or ethanol per gram powder was used. MIL-101 activation was conducted in a degassed oven at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ for further use.
xCuCoM-DS: xCuCoM-DS was obtained via a popular DS strategy with some modifications [46]. $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of activated MIL-101 was added into the hydrophobic solvent of dry $n$ -hexane ( $20~\mathrm{mL}$ , and the suspension was sonicated for $15\mathrm{min}$ until a uniform dispersion was aroused. After stirring for $2\mathrm{h}$ , $140~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of hydrophilic $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mixed water solution were added dropwise within $15~\mathrm{min}$ under persistent and vigorous stirring. Then, the solvent was poured off after further being stirred for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the collected powder was dried by degassing at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At last, the mixed powder was calcined in flowing argon $\mathrm{100mL{\cdot}m i n^{-1}}$ ) at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ at a heating speed of $2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The resulting sample was named as xCuCoM-DS.
yCuCoM-WI: yCuCoM-WI was obtained by the classical WI method. $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ crystals were completely dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. The activated MIL101 was then added, and the mixture was continuously stirred at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until the water was evaporated. The powder was collected and calcined in flowing argon $\mathrm{100\mL{\cdot}m i n^{-1}}$ ) at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ at a heating speed of $2\mathrm{\mathrm{~}^{\circ}C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The resulting sample was labelled as yCuCoM-WI.
## Characterization
Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer was adopted to perform XRD patterns. Cu Kα was applied as the radiation source. $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $40~\mathrm{mA}$ was set as the testing parameters, and the scanning range is $2-50^{\circ}$ . ICP-OES was measured on PerkinElmer Optima $7300\mathrm{DV}$ to have the $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ concentrations. XPS profiles was obtained from Escalab 250Xi. HRTEM, HAADF-STEM images and EDX elemental mapping were taken from FEI Titan G2 80-200 at $300\mathrm{kV}$ . $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms were tested at $77\mathrm{K}$ on a Belsorp-max analyzer. All the samples were degassed at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove impurities before analysis. The surface area was calculated according to the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller equation. FT-IR spectra were acquired on Agilent FTS2000 with KBr as the substrate.
## $\mathbf{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathbf{C_{2}H_{6}}$ adsorption test and selectivity calculation
The Belsorp-max analyzer was employed for the ethane and ethylene adsorption isotherms. Prior to testing, the samples were degassed at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . Before beginning of the testing, the temperature was precisely controlled and ensured it was stable for over half an hour by a thermostatic bath with circulating water. The ideal adsorbed solution theory, abbreviated as IAST, was employed for evaluating the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity [47, 48]. Specifically, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption isotherms were simulated by the Double Langmuir (DL) model (Eq. 1). Afterwards, the fitting parameters were substituted into the IAST formula (Eq. 2) to acquire $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ for the calculation of selectivity of $\mathrm{S}_{12}$ (Eq. 3).
$$
\begin{array}{c}{q=q\frac{K_{c}P}{{d}\Bar{F}_{I}+K_{c}P}+q\frac{K_{i}P}{{d}\Bar{I}+K_{i}P}}\\ {q_{c l}l n\left(I+\frac{K_{c}\Bar{y}_{i}P\Bar{P}_{i}}{x_{I}}\right)-q_{c2}l n\left[I+\frac{K_{c2}(I-y_{l})P_{i}}{I-x_{l}}\right]+q_{i l}l n\left(I+\frac{K_{i}\Bar{y}_{l}P\Bar{P}_{i}}{x_{I}}\right)-q_{i2}l n\left[I+\frac{K_{i2}(I-y_{l})P_{i}}{I-x_{l}}\right]=0}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{S_{I2}=\frac{x_{I}/x_{2}}{y_{I}/y_{2}}}\end{array}
$$
## Results and discussion
## Physicochemical properties of adsorbents
The XRD profiles of pristine MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples are shown in Figure 1. The featured peaks of MIL-101 at $2\theta$ of $3.29^{\circ}$ , $5.92^{\circ}$ , $8.48^{\circ}$ , $9.12^{\circ}$ , $10.38^{\circ}$ and $16.54^{\circ}$ coincide well with the reported results, illustrating that MIL-101 is synthesized in high purity and crystallinity [49, 50]. After $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ modification, the intensities of these peaks are inferior to the supporter MIL-101, and decrease gradually with the increasing content of $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ loading. This is due to the incorporation of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ , which declines the MIL-101 content. Besides, there is no distortion on the patterns of $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ decorated samples compared with the profile of MIL-101, suggesting that there is no collapse to the structure of MIL-101 after the decoration of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ and reduction at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Notably, a new peak at $28.4^{\circ}$ appears on the profiles of CuCoM-WI and CuCoM-SG, originating from CuCl (JCPDS no. 06- 0344). This suggests that, though the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ occurs in the samples of CuCoM-WI and CuCoM-SG, the obtained CuCl species tend to aggregate on MIL-101. The average particle size of CuCl was calculated according to the Scherrer formula, to be above $20\mathrm{nm}$ : the specific values are 24.3 nm for 1.5CuCoM-WI and $39.8\mathrm{nm}$ for 1.5CuCoM-SG. The higher values of $42.3\mathrm{nm}$ for 2.0CuCoMWI and $50.2\mathrm{nm}$ for 2.0CuCoM-SG are discovered as well. It is plausible to conclude that these large particles can hardly be accommodated inside the micropores, and would mainly locate at the exterior of MIL-101. To our surprise, the patterns of CoM-DS, CuM-DS and CuCoM-DS show no XRD signals of metal sites, however, the concentrations of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ can be quantified by ICP-OES techniques (Table 1), suggesting the excellent dispersion of metal species at the interior of MIL-101.
The $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples were comprehensively analyzed by XPS studies, and the spectra are depicted in Figure 2. The signals in the region from 930 to $940~\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak, and the Cu $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ satellite peak present from 940 to $946~\mathrm{eV}$ is the feature of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ [51, 52]. As can be seen on the spectra of CuCoM-DS, there is a prominent peak centered at $932.8~\mathrm{eV}$ , and no obvious satellite peak between 940 and $946\mathrm{eV}$ . This confirms that only ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ species are present because all the guest $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ are completely reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ in the CuCoM-DS samples. As for 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-SG, there are two peaks at 932.8 and higher $934.8~\mathrm{eV}$ , which can be identified as $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ , respectively. In addition, the obvious satellite peak of $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ in the region from 940 to $946~\mathrm{eV}$ verifies the coexistence of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ . This suggests that only partial $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ in the samples is capable to be reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ . With regard to the 1.5CuM-DS sample without $\mathrm{Co}$ species, a broad peak of $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $934.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ and a wide satellite peak of Cu $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ clearly witness the presence of only $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ . This demonstrates the failed reduction of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ without the assistance of Co species. It then concludes that the primary impetus for the transform of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ on MIL-101 can be ascribed to the auxiliary Co species. Although no XRD signals, the existence of $\mathrm{Co}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples can be confirmed by the $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}3/2$ and Co 2p1/2 peaks (Figure S1) [53, 54]. Moreover, the efficiency of reduction is further dependent on the modification protocols. By use of the conventional WI and SG methods, only partial $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ can be reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ . Significantly, DS route is capable of full reduction of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ .
The dispersion of decorated metal species were observed in depth by HRTEM. Figures 3A-C exhibit the HRTEM images of 1.5CuCoM-DS. The octahedral morphology can be distinguished, and is consistent with the supporter MIL-101. This confirms that the original framework of MIL-101 well retains after the modification of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ . Another interesting finding is that some obvious weeny dark spots are found on MIL-101. On the HAADF-STEM image (Figure 3D), the white spots become more striking and are found to overspread the whole MIL-101 as well. It should be pointed out that on the HAADF-STEM mapping images, not only the host Cr, but also the guest Co and Cu elements are homogeneously dispersed on MIL-101. This is in accord with the XRD results demonstrating the excellent dispersion of metal sites inside MIL-101.
As depicted in Figure 4, the textural information of MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples was obtained from the $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms. The supporter displays a sharp increase at the low relative pressure region. According to the IUPAC nomenclature, this conforms to the type-I isotherm. Furthermore, there are secondary uptakes at the relative pressure close to 0.1 and 0.2, suggesting the emergence of two kinds of microporous windows. This structure is identical to the literature [55, 56]. After $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ modification, it is discovered that the types of the isotherms are identical to MIL-101, confirming the intact structure of the supporter. Nevertheless, the $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples gradually decrease as the increase of metal species, probably due to the triumphant introduction of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ on MIL-101. The pore parameters of MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples are displayed in Table 1. MIL-101 exhibits the values of surface area and pore volume to be 2634 $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $1.64\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. After $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ modification by the DS method, both surface areas and pore volumes descend to that $2000\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.06\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 1.5CuM-DS, $1827\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 1.04 $\mathrm{\mL{g}^{-1}}$ for 1.5CoM-DS, respectively. As the content of dual $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ sites aggrandizes, the surface areas and pore volumes decrease from $1944{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $1.17\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 0.5CuCoM-DS to $1518\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.90\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 2.0CuCoM-DS. This should be attributed to the introduction of $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ , which occupy the pores of the supporter MIL-101. Another intriguing result is that different methods of introducing $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ give rise to diverse surface areas and pore volumes. 1.5CuCoM-DS are $1698\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 1.03 $\mathrm{\mL{g}^{-1}}$ , while slightly lower values of $1674~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1.00\ \mathrm{\mL{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ for 1.5CuCoM-WI, 1563 $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{and}0{.}93~\mathbf{m}\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 1.5CuCoM-SG. The poor dispersion of metal sites in 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-SG is the possible reason for the inferior pore parameters.
FT-IR spectra of MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples are displayed in Figure 5. The IR spectrum of the supporter MIL-101 exhibits the identical profile to that in the literature [57, 58]. The sharp and intense peaks in the range of $1400-1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the stretching vibrations of $\mathrm{C=C}$ bond of aromatic ring. The weak peaks at 1017 and $749\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to distortion vibration δ(C-H) and bending vibration γ(C-H) of benzene ring, respectively. The peak at $585~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can originate from Cr-O vibrations. In addition, the broad peak around the $3500~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the water molecule or the acidic O-H of carbonyl group. It is worthwhile mentioning that the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples are alike to MIL-101, implying that the incorporation of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ has no
damage to the pristine structure of MIL-101.
Taking the above physicochemical features into account, it is rational to make some conclusions. (i) There is no structural collapse to the supporter after the decoration of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ on MIL-101. The similar XRD profiles, $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms and FT-IR spectra with the host, in addition to the fine octahedral morphology presented on HRTEM images, can collectively provide sufficient evidences. (ii) The Co species should be the decisive factor for driving the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ . On the basis of XRD and XPS results, in the case of 1.5CuM-DS without Co species, $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ can hardly be reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ . When referring to the samples of CuCoM-DS, CuCoM-WI and CuCoM-SG containing Co species, the conversion of $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ is feasible, leading to the generation of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ . It is commonly known that the conversion of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ needs to be operated at the temperature as high a $s>450^{\mathrm{~o~C~}}$ , however, such a high temperature will cause damages to MIL-101. Herein, at the temperature of $250^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ , not only the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ to $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ can be achieved, but also the structure of the host is well maintained. (iii) The reduction efficiency is closely dependent on the location of the metal compounds. In terms of 1.5CuCoM-DS, no signals of metal species on XRD patterns, overspread dots on HRTEM images and homogenous $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ elements on mapping imply that the metal sites are highly dispersed at the interior of MIL-101. When it comes to 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-SG, CuCl particles are as large as $>20$ nm, and mainly locate at the surface of the host. Notably, among these samples, only the copper species at the interior of MIL-101 (1.5CuCoM-DS) can reach the complete reduction to $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ . Those copper species at the exterior of MIL-101 (1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-SG) only undergo incomplete
reduction.
The successful control of metal species within the nanopores of MIL-101 should be credited to the DS strategy. The hydrophobic $n$ -hexane is substantial and likely to surround the outside of MIL-101. The solution of mixed metal salts is hydrophilic in a small quantity of $140\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , which is slightly inferior to the $\mathrm{V_{p}}$ of used MIL-101 ( $164\upmu\mathrm{L}$ for $0.1\ \mathrm{g}$ , Table 1). As a result, the metallic solution can be entirely accommodated within the hydrophilic inner space of nanoporous MIL-101 under the capillary force. Owing to the confinement effect of the nanopores provided by the host, metal species can avoid aggregation and form the outstanding dispersion. With regard to the WI method, the solution of mixed metal salts is in a large quantity of $10~\mathrm{mL}$ . Due to the limited inner space, only partial can go inside the nanopores, and the majority would locate at the outer surface of the supporter. As concerning to the SG mean, the metallic crystals are ground into powders. However, the sizes of these grains are still too large to be driven into the pores in nanometers.
## $\mathbf{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathbf{C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation performances
Figure 6 exhibits the adsorption isotherms of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ on the supporter and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ decorated samples at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The adsorption capacities of the host MIL-101 for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ are $48.1\ \mathrm{mL{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ and $45.1\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ , respectively. The uptakes are ascribed to the physical adsorption. Possibly due to the interaction between $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , the adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is slightly higher than that of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ . However, the discrepancy is feeble, which makes pure MIL-101 ineffective for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ . At $100\mathrm{kPa}$ , the adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ are $41.6,41.2\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 1.5CoM-DS and $38.8,36.7~\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 1.5CuM-DS, respectively. The capacities are lower than those on MIL-101, which is probably due to the reduced surface areas. It is worth noting that the uptake of 1.5CuCoM-DS for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ reaches up to $50.5\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ , while the adsorption for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ drops down to $33.2~\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The enhanced $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake implies a strong interaction between the adsorbent and gas, thanks to the $\pi$ -complexation bond between $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ in 1.5CuCoM-DS and $\mathrm{C=C}$ in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . Otherwise, the formation of Van der Waals’ force between 1.5CuCoM-DS and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is deduced, and its bond energy is lower than the $\pi$ -complexation interaction, which should be the reason for the inferior capacity. CuCoM-DS analogues with various metal loading (0.5, 1.0 and 2.0) show the enhanced $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ capacities than ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ as well (Figure S2), and the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptakes are lower than that of 1.5CuCoM-DS. When it comes to the 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-SG samples, the adsorbed amount is 45.5, 40.9 $\mathrm{\mL{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and $34.2,34.1\mathrm{mL}\mathbf{\cdotg}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ , respectively. Both the two adsorbents show disparity between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ uptakes. However, the gap is found to be narrower than 1.5CuCoM-DS, which suggests the inferior separation performances compared with 1.5CuCoM-DS.
With regard to practical applications, not only the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake, but also the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity plays a critical role. The DL model was used to fit the adsorption isotherms of single component of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , respectively, as can be seen in Figures S3 and S4. The obtained fitting parameters (Table S1) were adopted for the further calculation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity by the IAST method. Figure 7 describes the calculated selectivity at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ . As depicted, the selectivity of 1.5CoM-DS and 1.5CuM-DS are lower than the host MIL-101 in the whole $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molar fraction range. Notably, the profiles of 1.5CuCoM-SG, 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-DS are shown to be above MIL-101, indicating superior selectivity to the host. To be specific, at a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molar fraction of 0.5, the selectivity of MIL-101, 1.5CuCoM-SG, 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-DS increase from 1.1 to 1.7, 2.3 and 2.6. The corresponding selectivity values of analogue CuCoM-DS samples are 1.2, 2.0 and 2.5 for the prefix numbers of 0.5, 1.0 and 2.0 before sample names, respectively (Figure S5). On the basis of these results, we can conclude that with overall consideration of both uptake and selectivity, among the serial CuCoM-DS samples, 1.5CuCoM-DS is best. Moreover, the selectivity of 1.5CuCoM-DS surpasses not only MIL-101 and 1.5CuM-DS, but also other dual sites samples of 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoMSG.
The enhanced performance of the 1.5CuCoM-DS adsorbent, including $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption capacity and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity, is probably owing to the plentiful $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ , which is generally accepted to be capable to arouse the $\pi$ -complexation interaction with $\mathrm{C=C}$ bond in $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . In order to further explore the bond strength, the isosteric heat of adsorption $\left(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\right)$ was calculated according to the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ isotherms at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 6) and $35^{\mathrm{{\circ}C}}$ (Figure S6). The essential parameters were obtained from simulating the isotherms at $35~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ with DL model (Figure S6, Table S2), along with the aforementioned results at 25 $^\mathrm{{{o}C}}$ (Figure S3, Table S1), then the Clausius−Clapeyron equation [44] was employed for the calculation. As displayed in Figure 8, the $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ of every adsorbent gradually decreases with the rise of the pressure, which is resulted from the coverage of adsorption sites. At zero-coverage, the $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ of pure supporter is $9.0\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , which is in accord with the physical adsorption as described above. It is vital to point out that the $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ decorated samples presented enhanced $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ at zero-coverage, which are 42.0, 54.4 and $67.3~\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for 1.5CuCoM-SG, 1.5CuCoM-WI and 1.5CuCoM-DS, respectively. Each value is superior to that of physical bond $(40\mathrm{kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ) and inferior to that of chemical interaction $(84\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})}.$ ). The results verify the proposed $\pi$ -complexation interaction, whose bond energy is stronger than Van der Waals’ force. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ at zero-coverage increases in the order of $1.5\mathrm{CuCoM-SG}<$ $1.5\mathrm{CuCoM-WI}<1.5\mathrm{CuCoM-DS},$ , in good consistence with the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation performance.
## Stability of ${{\mathbf{Cu}}^{\mathbf{I}}}$ sites in adsorbents
With respect to the practical applications, stability is a matter of cardinal significance. In humid air, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ species can be quickly transformed into $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ compounds and then have no activity to form $\pi$ - complexation interactions with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . In order to investigate the oxygen resistance, 1.5CuCoM-DS was exposed to atmospheric air for a certain period. As exhibited in Figure 9A, the fresh 1.5CuCoM-DS can adsorb $50.5\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ . After exposure for 7 days, the quantity of absorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ slightly decreases to $48.7\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , as $96.4\%$ of the fresh uptake. Upon 12-day exposure, the adsorbent is still capable of capturing $46.6\mathrm{mL}\mathbf{\cdotg}^{-1}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , which is $92.3\%$ of the fresh capacity. Further exposure up to 15 days, the adsorption capacity is reduced to $82.8\%$ $(41.8~\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) of the fresh amount. It is worthy noting that over $90\%$ of fresh capacity of 1.5CuCoM-DS is maintained after 12 days of exposure to atmospheric air, which is competent for the practical application. To achieve a better understanding of the plausible reason for declining uptakes, XPS studies of $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}$ regions are shown in Figure S7. It can be seen that there is one $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ peak in the fresh sample. After exposure to atmospheric air, another $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ peak emerges and gradually becomes strengthen as the time increases. Nevertheless, within 12 days, the predominant component is still the active $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{I}}}$ species. However, unfavorable $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ species occupy a predominant proportion when it is up to 15 days. The repeatability is an important feature for adsorbents as well. Therefore, the regeneration performance of 1.5CuCoM-DS was also investigated. After each cycle, the used adsorbent was degassed at $150^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . As shown in Figure
S8, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ uptake after 5 cycles shows no decline, indicating that 1.5CuCoM-DS has excellent repeatability.
## Conclusions
In summary, Confining the location of Cu sites within MIL-101 with simultaneous Co species doping was demonstrated to be a competing strategy to achieve both complete reduction and excellent dispersion of $\mathrm{Cu^{I}}$ on MIL-101 (CuCoM-DS). When the metal sites are at the exterior of MIL-101, such as in CuCoM-WI and CuCoM-SG, $\mathrm{Cu^{\mathrm{II}}}$ cannot be completely reduced to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ and tend to form aggregation. While in the absence of Co, even at the interior of MIL-101, $\mathrm{{Cu^{\mathrm{{II}}}}}$ in CuM-DS can hardly be reduced. The well dispersed ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ species greatly contribute to forming the $\pi$ -complexation bonds with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and the optimal 1.5CuCoM-DS shows an enhanced $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption uptake and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity in comparison with the host MIL-101 and counterpart samples of 1.5CuCoM-WI, 1.5CuCoM-SG, 1.5CuM-DS. More interesting result is the 12-days stability, as revealed on 1.5CuCoM-DS. This study offers a potential avenue to improve the reduction efficiency and dispersion degree of the guest cuprous species on MOFs.
